/* Track: When it does not land. Ordered foundational first.
 *
 * Escalation is the part of this subject with the most folklore and the least
 * preparation. Almost nobody writes down their own criteria in advance, which
 * means the decision gets made under pressure by the person paying the cost. */
(window.LESSONS = window.LESSONS || []).push(
{
  id: "escalation-stop-work-authority",
  track: "escalation", level: "everyday",
  title: "Somebody has to be able to stop the line, and it has to be settled before the day",
  source: "Stop-work authority as practised in nuclear, aviation and heavy industry, and the andon cord in the Toyota Production System",
  gateIntro: "The deploy is halfway through and you have seen something in the logs you cannot explain. Continuing takes about four minutes. Stopping means an incomplete rollout across two regions and probably an hour of work to unwind. Nobody has ever stopped one of these midway. You do not know whether you are allowed to.",
  gatePrompt: "You are going to stop it. Write what you say in the channel, in the next fifteen seconds.",
  evidence: "No trial. High-hazard industries grant explicit stop authority to individual workers, with the obligation on management to make stopping cost-free, because leaving the decision to be made under pressure by the person who bears the social cost produces systematic non-stopping. Toyota's andon system is the best-known implementation. The evidence is operational adoption across industries with strong incentives to get it right, not experiment.",
  replication: "craft",
  idea: "Establish who can stop a running process, in advance and in writing, because a stop decided in the moment is made by the person paying the full social cost of stopping and they will systematically not pay it.",
  why: "The insight in the andon system is not that workers should be brave. It is that the organisation removed the reason not to pull. The cord is expected to be pulled, pulling it is not an accusation, and a supervisor arrives to help rather than to ask why. Every part of that is a design decision aimed at making the cost of a false stop lower than the cost of a missed one, because the default arrangement has those the other way round.\n\nIn most software teams the arrangement is the default one. Stopping a deploy is unusual, it inconveniences people visibly, and the person stopping it has to justify themselves immediately while the person who continued through a bad signal never has to justify anything. Under that arrangement a rational person continues, and no amount of talking about ownership changes the arithmetic. The fix is to settle it beforehand: name who can stop, state that no justification is required at the time, and commit that the review afterwards is about the signal rather than the stopper.",
  failureMode: "You continue because stopping feels like it needs authorisation you do not have. The four minutes complete, the unexplained log line turns out to be the first symptom, and the incident that follows is much larger than the hour of unwinding would have been. In the review, everybody agrees you should have stopped, which was not the information available to you at the time.",
  worked: "Stop first and put the reasoning second, because the reasoning takes longer than the window:\n\n\"Stopping the deploy. Region two is halted, region one is partially through.\n\nReason: I am seeing connection resets on the write path that I cannot account for, starting about ninety seconds ago. It may be nothing. I would rather unwind an hour of work than find out at full rollout.\n\nI am not asking anyone to approve this, I am telling you it is stopped. Happy to be told I was wrong once we know.\"\n\nI am not asking anyone to approve this is the part that has to be in the first message. If you ask, you have created a window in which somebody says continue.",
  experiment: "Ask your team, out loud, who is allowed to stop a deploy midway and whether they need to ask first. If the answers differ, you have found a gap that will be resolved badly during an incident. Write the answer down and put it where the runbook is.",
  reflection: "On your team, who can stop a running process without asking, and does everyone give the same answer?",
  recall: {
    q: "What does an andon cord actually change, and why does an in-the-moment stop decision fail systematically?",
    a: "It removes the reasons not to pull: pulling is expected, carries no accusation, and brings help rather than interrogation, which makes a false stop cheaper than a missed one. In-the-moment decisions fail because the default arrangement is the reverse, with the stopper justifying themselves immediately while whoever continued through a bad signal never has to, so a rational person continues."
  },
  deepDive: "Help me write a stop-work policy for my team covering who can halt a deploy, whether they must ask, and how the review afterwards works."
},
{
  id: "escalation-trigger-in-advance",
  track: "escalation", level: "costly",
  title: "Decide what would make you escalate before you are the person who has to",
  source: "Standard practice in compliance and safety functions, where escalation criteria are defined centrally rather than left to individual judgement",
  gateIntro: "You have raised the same integration risk three times over five weeks. Each time it has been acknowledged and not actioned. You are now wondering whether this is escalation-worthy or whether you are being difficult, and you have been wondering that for two of the five weeks. The person you would escalate to is friendly and busy.",
  gatePrompt: "Write your escalation criteria for this issue, as a rule, in one sentence.",
  evidence: "No trial. Compliance, audit and safety functions define escalation thresholds centrally and in advance, on the explicit reasoning that individuals under social pressure systematically under-escalate and that leaving the criterion to in-the-moment judgement produces the wrong answer reliably. This is an institutional design assumption with a long track record rather than a measured effect.",
  replication: "craft",
  idea: "Write the escalation trigger down while the question is still hypothetical, because deciding in the moment means deciding while paying the social cost, and that decision is reliably biased toward waiting.",
  why: "The whole reason compliance functions define thresholds centrally is that individual judgement in the moment is known to be biased in one direction. It is not that people are cowardly. It is that the costs are asymmetric in time: escalating is immediately and personally unpleasant, and not escalating is costly later and diffusely. Anybody weighing those in the moment weighs them from inside the unpleasant half.\n\nWriting a trigger converts the decision into a much easier one. Instead of asking is this bad enough, which has no answer and which you have now been asking for two weeks, you ask whether the condition you set has been met, which has an answer. The trigger should be mechanical: a number of attempts, a date, or a specific event. After three asks with no action, or past the fifteenth, I escalate. The value is not that the threshold is correct, because you cannot know that. The value is that it was set by a version of you who was not paying the cost.",
  failureMode: "You keep asking yourself whether it is bad enough. It stays not quite bad enough for another three weeks, and then it becomes obviously bad enough at the moment it materialises, which is the one moment escalating is worth nothing.",
  worked: "The trigger is a sentence you write for yourself, and it looks like this:\n\n\"If the integration risk has not been actioned by the twenty-second, or if I have raised it a fourth time without a named owner, I take it to Dana. Not conditional on how bad it looks then.\"\n\nThe last clause is the load-bearing one. Without it you will re-evaluate the severity when the date arrives, from inside the same social cost, and reach the same answer you have been reaching for five weeks.",
  experiment: "Write triggers for the three things you are currently carrying that might need escalating. Give them dates. Then put the dates in a calendar, because a trigger you have to remember to check is a trigger you will re-evaluate rather than obey.",
  reflection: "What are you currently wondering whether to escalate, and how long have you been wondering?",
  recall: {
    q: "Why do compliance functions define escalation thresholds centrally, and what makes a good personal trigger?",
    a: "Because in-the-moment individual judgement is biased in one direction: escalating is immediately and personally unpleasant while not escalating is costly later and diffusely, so anyone deciding is deciding from inside the unpleasant half. A good trigger is mechanical, a count or a date or a specific event, and includes an explicit clause that it is not conditional on how bad things look when it fires."
  },
  deepDive: "Here are three things I am carrying that might need escalating. Help me write a mechanical trigger with a date for each."
},
{
  id: "escalation-bystander-is-contested",
  track: "escalation", level: "costly",
  title: "The famous story about nobody helping was largely untrue, and the real-world picture may run the other way",
  source: "The 1964 Genovese case reporting and its later correction, Manning, Levine and Collins (American Psychologist, 2007), and Philpot and colleagues' analysis of CCTV footage (American Psychologist, 2020)",
  gateIntro: "A serious problem has been visible in a shared dashboard for a week. Six teams can see it. You have assumed one of the others owns it, and you have just learned in a corridor that at least two of the other teams made the same assumption. Nobody has raised it. A colleague has just described this to you as a classic bystander effect and moved on satisfied.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the message that claims this rather than diagnosing it.",
  evidence: "The account of thirty-eight witnesses who watched and did nothing was substantially inaccurate, and the newspaper that published it later acknowledged as much. Laboratory work on diffusion of responsibility has replicated reasonably well and a meta-analysis supports a real effect under some conditions. Against that, an analysis of CCTV footage of real public conflicts across three countries found intervention in around nine in ten incidents, with more bystanders associated with a higher chance of help rather than a lower one.",
  replication: "contested",
  idea: "Treat the shared-visibility problem as real without accepting the popular account of it, and resolve it the only way that works, which is by claiming the item publicly rather than by naming the phenomenon.",
  why: "The evidential position here is genuinely mixed and worth holding accurately, because this is a case where the folklore and the correction are both overstated. The founding story was wrong in its particulars. The laboratory effect is real and has replicated. And the best available evidence from real emergencies points the other way on group size, which is a substantial complication that most people repeating the story have never encountered.\n\nWhat survives for your situation is narrower and more mundane than the famous version: when responsibility is ambiguous, people assume it sits elsewhere, and they do so in good faith. That does not require any special psychology and it is not a moral failing. It also does not need a name. Naming it, which is what your colleague did, produces the same satisfied closure as naming groupthink, and it substitutes for the only thing that resolves it, which is somebody attaching their name to the item. The diagnosis is free and the claim costs something, which is why the diagnosis is more popular.",
  failureMode: "The phenomenon gets named and everybody agrees it is happening, which feels like progress. Nobody claims the item, because naming it did not change whose it is, and it stays on the dashboard for another fortnight with six teams now consciously aware that nobody owns it.",
  worked: "Claim it, name a date, and explicitly release the other teams:\n\n\"I am taking the connection leak on the shared dashboard. It has been visible for a week and I think at least three of us have each assumed somebody else had it, including me.\n\nI will have a diagnosis by Thursday. If it turns out to belong to another team I will hand it over with what I have found. Nobody else needs to pick this up, and if you were about to, tell me and I will drop it.\"\n\nExplicitly releasing the others is not a courtesy. Without it you have added a seventh assumption to the pile, which is that somebody has claimed it and therefore it is handled, and that is how items get claimed twice or not at all.",
  experiment: "Look at any shared dashboard or alert channel your team can see and find the item that has been red longest. Ask, in the channel, who owns it. The answer is frequently that nobody does, and asking is a smaller act than claiming while producing most of the same effect.",
  reflection: "What has been visible to several teams for weeks with nobody owning it, and have you assumed it was somebody else's?",
  recall: {
    q: "What is the actual evidential position on the bystander effect, and what resolves the workplace version?",
    a: "The founding case was substantially misreported, the laboratory effect has replicated reasonably well, and CCTV analysis of real conflicts found help in around nine in ten incidents with more bystanders associated with more help. What survives is that ambiguous responsibility leads people to assume it sits elsewhere in good faith, and it is resolved by somebody publicly claiming the item with a date, not by naming the phenomenon."
  },
  deepDive: "Something has been visible to several teams for weeks with no owner. Write the message where I claim it and release the others."
},
{
  id: "escalation-one-rung-at-a-time",
  track: "escalation", level: "costly",
  title: "Go up one level, tell the level you left, and give each rung a real chance",
  source: "Escalation convention in operations and management practice, where skipping levels is treated as a distinct failure from escalating",
  gateIntro: "Your team lead has not acted on a problem you raised twice. The person who could fix it in an afternoon is three levels up and you have their number from an unrelated project. Going straight to them would probably work. You have also seen what happened to someone else on a neighbouring team who did something similar in March.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the message to the next rung up, not the third.",
  evidence: "No trial. The one-rung convention is standard operational practice and its rationale is procedural: each level has information the level below lacks, skipping removes the opportunity for a cheaper resolution, and the skipped level loses both the chance to act and the standing to. Nothing measures whether one-rung escalation resolves more issues than skipping, and the convention persists on relationship grounds as much as effectiveness ones.",
  replication: "craft",
  idea: "Escalate to the next level rather than the level that can fix it, and tell the person you are going past, because the skip is what gets remembered and the intermediate rung frequently resolves it anyway.",
  why: "Skipping is tempting because it is efficient, and efficiency is the wrong objective in a process whose main cost is relational. The three-level jump probably does fix this problem. It also tells your team lead and their manager that you route around them, which is a fact about you that will be applied to every future issue, and it removes your ability to escalate cheaply next time because the cheap rungs no longer trust you.\n\nThe procedural reason is separate and also real. The intermediate level often has context you do not: your team lead may have raised it, may have been told to wait, or may know something about the timing. Going one rung gives that information a chance to surface. And each rung you pass through in the normal way strengthens the escalation, because arriving at level three having been through levels one and two is a much stronger position than arriving cold. If it genuinely needs the third level, the second level will usually take it there themselves, which is faster than your direct message and costs you nothing.",
  failureMode: "You go to the third level. The problem is fixed in an afternoon, which confirms your judgement, and two people now know you skipped them. The next time you raise something at level one it is received by somebody managing the risk that you will go over their head again, which changes how they respond to you permanently.",
  worked: "Next rung, with the history stated plainly and the notification to the level you are leaving:\n\n\"I have raised the connection pool issue with Marcus twice and it has not moved, so I am bringing it to you, and I have told Marcus I am doing that.\n\nThe issue is that the pool exhausts under retry storms and needs a restart to recover. We have had it four times since March. What I am asking for is a decision on whether this gets scheduled, because I do not think it fits in the gaps.\n\nI am not raising a problem with Marcus and I do not think he has handled this badly. I think it needs somebody who can make room for it.\"\n\nThe last paragraph is worth writing every time. It is usually true, and omitting it means the escalation reads as a complaint about a person rather than a request for a decision.",
  experiment: "Before your next escalation, write down what the intermediate level might know that you do not. If you can construct two plausible items, go through them. If you genuinely cannot construct any, that is a signal that you have not talked to them enough rather than that they have nothing.",
  reflection: "When did you last skip a level, and what did the people you skipped conclude about you?",
  recall: {
    q: "Why is skipping levels a distinct failure from escalating, and what does going through each rung buy you?",
    a: "Because the main cost is relational rather than procedural: the skip is a fact about you that gets applied to every future issue and removes cheap escalation routes, even when the jump solves this problem. Going through each rung surfaces context you lack, and arriving at a higher level having passed through the lower ones is a much stronger position than arriving cold."
  },
  deepDive: "I need to escalate past my team lead. Write the message to the next level up and the notification to the person I am going past."
},
{
  id: "escalation-sunk-cost-in-the-listener",
  track: "escalation", level: "costly",
  title: "The person you are escalating to may be the person who chose it",
  source: "Staw's work on escalation of commitment (Organizational Behavior and Human Performance, 1976) and the subsequent meta-analytic literature",
  gateIntro: "You are about to escalate a problem with the platform migration to the director who sponsored it, approved the budget, and presented it to the board in February. Your case is that it is not working and should be stopped. You have been treating this as a question of whether your evidence is strong enough.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the opening, given that the person deciding is the person who chose it.",
  evidence: "Decision makers who were personally responsible for an initial allocation committed more additional resources to a failing course of action than those who inherited the same situation, and the effect has been reproduced across many studies and settings. A later meta-analysis identified personal responsibility for the original decision as one of the more consistent moderators. The finding is well established; the size varies considerably with context, and most of the work is laboratory or scenario-based.",
  replication: "replicated",
  idea: "Check whether your escalation target made the original decision, because personal responsibility for a commitment reliably increases willingness to spend more on it, and if it does you need a route that is not asking them to reverse themselves.",
  why: "The escalation-of-commitment result is one of the more reliable in this area and it has a direct operational consequence that people rarely act on. If the person who has to decide is the person who chose, your evidence is not being evaluated by a neutral party. It is being evaluated by someone whose responsibility for the original allocation makes additional commitment more likely, and the effect is largest exactly where you need it to be smallest.\n\nThis does not mean you cannot escalate to them. It means the ask has to be shaped around it. Asking them to stop the programme is asking for a reversal of their own decision, which is the request most affected. Asking for a checkpoint with pre-agreed criteria is a different request that does not require them to conclude anything about the past: they can agree to a test while still believing the programme will pass it. If it fails the test, the decision to stop is then made against a criterion they set, which is a much easier thing for them to do than accepting your conclusion now.",
  failureMode: "You escalate with a strong case for stopping. It is met with a request for more evidence, then with a plan to address the concerns, then with additional resource to get it back on track. Each of those is a reasonable-sounding response and the cumulative effect is more commitment, which is the documented pattern rather than a coincidence.",
  worked: "Do not ask for the reversal. Ask for the criterion, and let the criterion do the work:\n\n\"I want to raise where the migration has got to, and I am deliberately not asking you to stop it, because I do not think that is the right decision to be making today.\n\nWhat I am asking for is a checkpoint. Pick a date and two numbers that would tell us this is working: I would suggest tenants migrated and rollback time. If we hit them, I will stop raising this and I will say so publicly. If we do not, we have a decision to make against a bar you set rather than against my opinion.\"\n\nSetting the bar themselves is the part that makes this acceptable, and it is also what makes the eventual stop possible, because failing your own criterion is a very different act from accepting somebody else's argument.",
  experiment: "For your next escalation, write down who made the original decision and how publicly. If it is your escalation target and it was public, plan for a criterion rather than a conclusion before you write anything else.",
  reflection: "Whose decision are you asking to be reversed, and are you asking the person who made it?",
  recall: {
    q: "What does the escalation of commitment finding predict about your escalation target, and how should the ask be reshaped?",
    a: "That someone personally responsible for the original allocation will commit more additional resource to a failing course than someone who inherited it, so your evidence is being weighed by a party the effect acts on most strongly. Reshape the ask from a reversal to a checkpoint with criteria they set themselves, since failing your own bar is much easier to act on than accepting someone else's conclusion."
  },
  deepDive: "I need to escalate a failing project to the person who sponsored it. Help me design the checkpoint and criteria rather than the argument to stop."
},
{
  id: "escalation-ask-for-the-reversible",
  track: "escalation", level: "costly",
  title: "Ask for the version that can be undone, because that is a request people grant",
  source: "Practice in change and release management, where reversibility is treated as the primary control on decisions made under uncertainty",
  gateIntro: "You believe the data model change is wrong. You have argued it twice and lost, and you are aware you may be wrong yourself, at maybe forty per cent confident. The change ships next week. Continuing to argue for not shipping has failed twice and there is no new information to bring.",
  gatePrompt: "Stop arguing for the outcome. Write the ask for something reversible instead.",
  evidence: "No trial. Reversibility as the primary control under uncertainty is standard in release engineering, in surgical and clinical decision-making, and in policy design, on the reasoning that when you cannot resolve the disagreement you can bound its cost. There is nothing to measure and the principle is not in dispute; what is unevidenced is the claim that framing an objection this way changes how often it is granted.",
  replication: "craft",
  idea: "When the argument has failed, stop asking for the outcome and ask for reversibility, because it is a much smaller request and it makes being wrong survivable for both of you.",
  why: "You have lost twice and have no new information, so a third argument for the same outcome is not a plan. What has not been tried is a different request. Reversibility is smaller, it does not require anybody to concede the substantive point, and it is often cheap: a flag, a rehearsal in staging, a reverse migration script, a phased rollout. Someone who is confident the change is right has no reason to refuse any of those, because in their model the safeguard costs a day and is never used.\n\nThat is precisely why it is grantable. You are not asking them to entertain the possibility that they are wrong in any way that costs them something. You are asking for insurance whose premium is small, and their confidence is the argument for the premium being wasted rather than against it being paid. There is a subtler benefit too: a reversible change that is later reversed produces a clean, uncontested record of what happened, whereas an irreversible one that goes wrong produces a fight about whether it was the change at all.",
  failureMode: "You argue a third time for not shipping. It fails, as the first two did, and you have now spent the credibility that would have bought you the flag. The change ships with no way back, and when the problem appears there is a week of argument about whether it was the schema before anybody can act.",
  worked: "Concede the argument explicitly, then make the small ask, and price it for them:\n\n\"I have argued this twice and lost twice and I am not going to argue it again. I am about forty per cent confident I am right, which is not enough to hold up a release.\n\nWhat I want instead is a reverse migration script and one rehearsal of it against a copy of production. That is a day of my time, not yours. If you are right, we have wasted a day. If I am right, that day is the difference between a rollback and a week of fixing forward. I will do the work.\"\n\nOffering to do the work yourself removes the last reason to refuse, and it is usually a genuine day rather than a rhetorical one.",
  experiment: "Take your last three lost arguments and write, for each, what the reversible version would have been. Most of them have one, most of them are under two days of work, and most of them were never asked for because the argument was still being fought.",
  reflection: "Which argument have you lost twice, and what is the reversibility you could ask for instead?",
  recall: {
    q: "Why is a request for reversibility grantable when the substantive argument has failed twice?",
    a: "Because it is a much smaller ask that requires no concession on the merits: someone confident the change is right sees the safeguard as a cheap premium that will never be claimed, so their confidence argues for granting it rather than against. It also produces a clean record, since a reversed change is uncontested while an irreversible failure starts an argument about causation."
  },
  deepDive: "I lost an argument about a risky change. Help me identify the cheapest reversible safeguard I could ask for instead."
},
{
  id: "escalation-burden-of-proof",
  track: "escalation", level: "line",
  title: "Ask them to show it is safe rather than accepting the job of showing it is not",
  source: "The safety case approach in regulated engineering, where the proponent carries the burden of demonstrating adequate safety",
  gateIntro: "You have raised a concern about the failover path. The response has been that there is no evidence it will fail, and that you have not demonstrated a specific scenario in which it does. You are now being asked to prove a negative about a system you do not own, using logs you do not have access to, before the concern will be taken seriously.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the sentence that moves the burden without refusing to do any work.",
  evidence: "No trial and none applicable. Regulated safety-critical industries require the operator to make a positive case that a system is adequately safe, rather than requiring a regulator to demonstrate that it is not. This is a legal and procedural design choice, adopted because the alternative allocation systematically favours proceeding, and its justification is structural rather than empirical.",
  replication: "craft",
  cheat: "\"What is the positive case that this fails over correctly?\" The proponent owes the demonstration, not the objector.",
  idea: "Ask for the positive case that the system works rather than accepting the burden of demonstrating that it fails, because the allocation of that burden usually decides the outcome before any evidence is examined.",
  why: "Whoever carries the burden loses when the evidence is inconclusive, which it usually is. That is why regulated industries do not ask the regulator to prove a plant unsafe. They require the operator to make a positive case, on the reasoning that the party who wants to proceed should demonstrate adequacy, and that the party with access to the evidence should be the one producing it. Both of those apply exactly to your situation, and the second one is decisive: you are being asked to prove something using data you cannot see.\n\nMoving the burden is not a debating trick and it should not be done as one. It is a question about what the team knows, and it is frequently productive in a way the argument was not, because the honest answer is often that nobody has tested the failover. That answer is far more useful than any scenario you could have constructed, and it is available in one question. The way to keep this from reading as evasion is to offer to help produce the evidence, which also demonstrates that you are after the answer rather than the point.",
  failureMode: "You accept the burden. You spend two weeks constructing a specific failure scenario from partial information, it contains one arguable assumption because it had to, and the whole concern is dismissed on that assumption. The failover has still never been tested and now the question is closed.",
  worked: "Reframe as a question about evidence, not about who is right, and offer to do the work:\n\n\"I do not think I can prove it fails, and I am not sure that is the right question. What I would like to see is the positive case that it works. Has the failover been exercised end to end, and if so when and what was the result?\n\nIf it has, I will drop this entirely. If it has not, then neither of us knows, and I would rather we found out on a Tuesday afternoon than during an incident. I am happy to run the test myself if someone gives me access.\"\n\nIf it has, I will drop this entirely is what makes the question safe to answer honestly, because it means a yes ends the conversation rather than inviting the next objection.",
  experiment: "The next time you are asked to prove a negative, ask instead what evidence exists for the positive. Keep a note of how often the honest answer turns out to be none. In most organisations the proportion is high enough to change how you treat the next reassurance you receive.",
  reflection: "What have you been asked to disprove recently, and does the positive evidence for it actually exist?",
  recall: {
    q: "Why does the allocation of burden usually decide the outcome, and how do you move it without it reading as evasion?",
    a: "Because whoever carries it loses when the evidence is inconclusive, which it usually is, and the objector typically lacks access to the data that would settle it. Move it by asking what positive evidence exists that the system works, committing to drop the concern if the answer is good, and offering to produce the evidence yourself."
  },
  deepDive: "I am being asked to prove a system will fail. Help me reframe this as a request for the positive safety case."
},
{
  id: "escalation-do-not-go-alone-twice",
  track: "escalation", level: "line",
  title: "Escalate alone once, and find a second name before you do it again",
  source: "The dissenting-partner findings applied to escalation, and the general convention that a repeated lone complaint is attributed to the complainant",
  gateIntro: "You escalated the resourcing problem to your director in April. It was heard and nothing changed. It is now June, the problem is worse, and you are drafting the second escalation. You are the only person who has ever raised it formally, though three colleagues have complained about it to you privately at length.",
  gatePrompt: "Before you send anything, write the ask you make of one of those three colleagues.",
  evidence: "No trial for the escalation case specifically. The relevant experimental result is that a single dissenting voice is far more easily discounted than two, established most clearly in the conformity variations. The organisational convention that a repeated lone complaint gets attributed to the person rather than the situation is practitioner observation, and it is consistent with ordinary attribution reasoning rather than derived from it.",
  replication: "craft",
  idea: "Get a second person onto the second escalation, because a lone voice raising the same thing twice shifts the explanation from the problem to the person raising it.",
  why: "The first escalation is about the issue. The second one from the same person, alone, becomes about that person, and the shift happens without anybody deciding it should. The reasoning on the receiving end is ordinary: if this were as serious as described, presumably somebody else would have mentioned it, and if nobody has, the most economical explanation involves the one person who keeps raising it. That inference is wrong in your case and it is not unreasonable.\n\nA second name breaks it completely, and the bar for what that person has to do is much lower than it feels. They do not need to co-author, argue, or take any risk you are not taking. They need to be named as someone who shares the concern, or better, to add two sentences of their own. Your three private complainers are the pool, and the reason none of them has raised it formally is that each of them is facing the same lone-voice problem you are. Asking directly converts a coordination failure into a joint escalation, and it is one conversation.",
  failureMode: "You send the second escalation alone. It is received as the resourcing thing again, from you, and handled with the patience reserved for known grievances. The three colleagues continue to complain privately, each believing they are corroborating you, and none of them appears anywhere the director can see.",
  worked: "Ask small, ask specifically, and make it easy to say yes:\n\n\"I am escalating the resourcing problem again next week. I raised it alone in April and nothing moved, and I think part of that is that it looks like one person's complaint.\n\nWhat I am asking is small: can I put your name on it as someone who shares the concern? You do not have to write anything or be in the meeting. If you would rather add two lines about the on-call load in your own words, that would be much stronger, but the name alone is enough and I will not be offended if that is where you want to stop.\"\n\nGiving them the smallest possible version, and saying explicitly that stopping there is fine, is what gets a yes from somebody who is worried about exposure.",
  experiment: "Look at anything you have escalated more than once alone. For each, list who has complained to you privately about the same thing. Then ask one of them for their name. The usual discovery is that people say yes readily and had simply never been asked.",
  reflection: "What have you escalated twice alone, and who has been complaining to you privately about it?",
  recall: {
    q: "What changes between the first and second lone escalation, and what is the minimum you need from a second person?",
    a: "The object shifts from the issue to the person, because the economical explanation for nobody else raising something serious involves the one person who keeps raising it, and that inference is made without anybody deciding to make it. The minimum is their name attached as sharing the concern, with no writing, no meeting and no risk beyond being listed."
  },
  deepDive: "I have escalated something twice alone. Help me write the ask to a colleague who has complained about it privately."
},
{
  id: "escalation-record-the-override",
  track: "escalation", level: "line",
  title: "When you are overruled, establish who overruled you and on what basis",
  source: "Practice in regulated and safety-critical settings, where an override of a technical recommendation is recorded with its author",
  gateIntro: "You have recommended against the release. The recommendation has been overruled in a meeting where four people spoke and no single person said they were making the call. The release is going ahead. You have about a minute before the meeting ends, and asking who decided this feels confrontational.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the question, in a form that is procedural rather than accusatory.",
  evidence: "No trial. Regulated environments require an override of a technical recommendation to be attributable, on the reasoning that a decision with no identifiable author cannot be reviewed and creates no accountability. The pattern of diffuse group overrides being unattributable afterwards is documented extensively in accident investigations, which is retrospective evidence of the cost rather than of the remedy.",
  replication: "craft",
  cheat: "\"Who is making this call, so I can record it accurately?\" Procedural, unrefusable, and it changes what happens next.",
  idea: "Ask who is taking the decision and record it by name, because a diffuse override cannot be reviewed afterwards and the act of naming an owner changes the decision more often than arguing would have.",
  why: "A group override has no author and therefore no accountability, and that is not usually anybody's intention. Four people spoke, a direction emerged, and nobody experienced themselves as making a call. Afterwards, if it goes wrong, there is no decision to review, only a meeting, and the investigation finds a collective process. That is precisely the outcome that makes organisations unable to learn, and it is why regulated settings insist on attribution.\n\nAsking is safer than it feels because it can be framed as a recording question rather than a challenge. Who is making this call so I can write it down is procedural, it is hard to refuse, and it is a question the chair should welcome. It also has a live effect that is easy to underestimate: being named as the person overriding a technical recommendation causes people to re-examine whether they want to be. A meaningful fraction of diffuse overrides do not survive somebody asking who is doing it, not because anybody objects but because nobody had noticed a decision was being made.",
  failureMode: "You say nothing. The release goes ahead and fails. The review finds a meeting in which concerns were discussed and a decision was reached, with no owner and no basis recorded, so the finding is that communication could have been better. Your recommendation is somewhere in the transcript, having been overruled by nobody in particular.",
  worked: "Procedural framing, one question, and a stated reason for asking:\n\n\"Before we close, I want to record this accurately. My recommendation was not to release, and we are releasing, which is fine and I will support it. Who is taking that decision, so I can write it down with a name against it? And is the basis that the risk is lower than I estimated, or that it is acceptable given the date? Those are different and I would like the record to say which.\"\n\nThe second question does as much work as the first. Forcing the distinction between disagreeing with your risk estimate and accepting the risk is what makes the record useful later, and people answer it honestly because both answers are legitimate.",
  experiment: "In the next meeting where a technical recommendation is overruled, ask the recording question. Note whether the decision survives it. In most organisations the question is asked so rarely that the first time is noticeable, which is itself a finding about your decision hygiene.",
  reflection: "When were you last overruled by a group, and could you name who decided?",
  recall: {
    q: "Why is a diffuse override worse than a named one, and what two things should the recording question establish?",
    a: "Because it has no author and therefore cannot be reviewed, so a later failure produces a finding about communication rather than about a decision, which is how organisations become unable to learn. The question should establish who is taking the decision by name, and whether the basis is disagreement with the risk estimate or acceptance of the risk, since those are different and only one is about the evidence."
  },
  deepDive: "I was overruled by a group with no clear decision maker. Write me the procedural question that establishes who decided and on what basis."
},
{
  id: "escalation-early-and-partial",
  track: "escalation", level: "costly",
  title: "Escalate while the information is incomplete, because complete information arrives after the decision",
  source: "Incident command practice, where early notification with partial information is the standing expectation",
  gateIntro: "Something is wrong with the payments reconciliation and you are about forty minutes into working out what. You have a shape but not a cause, you do not know the blast radius, and you cannot yet say whether customers are affected. Your instinct is to understand it before you tell anyone, because telling people now means answering questions you cannot answer.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the notification you send now, with everything you do not know stated as unknown.",
  evidence: "No trial. Incident command frameworks across emergency services and technology operations require early notification with explicitly incomplete information, on the reasoning that the value of a warning decays fast and that the people who need to prepare cannot start once the picture is complete. The convention is near-universal in operational practice and rests on operational experience rather than measurement.",
  replication: "craft",
  idea: "Notify while you still have unknowns, and state the unknowns explicitly as unknowns, because a warning's value decays faster than your understanding improves.",
  why: "The instinct to understand before reporting is professional and it is wrong in one specific way: it optimises for the quality of your report rather than for the usefulness of the warning. The people who would need to act on this, whoever handles customer communications, whoever can pull in someone who knows the reconciliation code, cannot begin until they know. Every minute you spend improving your picture is a minute they are not spending on their part, and their part frequently has a longer lead time than yours.\n\nWhat makes early notification bearable is stating the unknowns as unknowns rather than hedging around them. The fear is being asked questions you cannot answer, and the answer is to list those questions yourself, in advance, as open items. A notification that says here is what I know, here is what I do not know, and here is when I will next update is complete in the only sense that matters, and it stops anybody asking for the things on your unknown list because you have already told them those are unknown.",
  failureMode: "You spend ninety minutes reaching a full understanding. It is a good understanding. By the time you send it, three hours of customer impact have accumulated that support knew nothing about, and the first question is why nobody was told, which is a much worse conversation than the one about incomplete information would have been.",
  worked: "Three sections and a next-update time, and no hedging in any of them:\n\n\"Raising this now with an incomplete picture rather than waiting.\n\nWhat I know: payments reconciliation has been failing since about 09:20. Roughly two hundred transactions are in an unreconciled state.\n\nWhat I do not know: the cause, whether it is still happening, whether any customer has seen a wrong balance, and whether this is recoverable by rerunning.\n\nWhat I am doing: working on cause. Next update at 11:30 whether or not I have one. If someone can check the customer-visible side in parallel, that would be the most useful thing right now.\"\n\nNext update whether or not I have one is what prevents the silence being interpreted, and it is the clause most people leave out.",
  experiment: "Set yourself a rule: anything that might be customer-affecting gets a notification within twenty minutes of you noticing it, with unknowns listed. Run it for a quarter and count how many were false alarms. Then compare that cost with the cost of the one that was not.",
  reflection: "What did you last investigate for an hour before telling anyone, and who could have started their part sooner?",
  recall: {
    q: "Why does waiting to understand before escalating optimise the wrong thing, and what makes early notification bearable?",
    a: "It optimises the quality of your report rather than the usefulness of the warning, while the people who must act cannot start until told and often have longer lead times than you do. It is made bearable by listing the unknowns explicitly as unknowns and committing to a next update time regardless of progress, which pre-empts the questions you cannot answer."
  },
  deepDive: "Something is wrong and I do not understand it yet. Help me write the early notification with the unknowns stated properly."
},
{
  id: "escalation-find-the-person",
  track: "escalation", level: "costly",
  title: "Processes absorb escalations, so find out which human the process routes to",
  source: "Observation of escalation paths in organisations, and the general operational preference for named owners over queues",
  gateIntro: "You have filed the concern through the official channel. It has a ticket number, it has been triaged, and it has a priority label that you now understand means it will be looked at within ninety days. The thing you are worried about happens in about three weeks. The process has technically worked and has produced nothing.",
  gatePrompt: "The ticket exists. Write the message to a person, and say why you are going around the queue.",
  evidence: "No trial. That formal intake processes convert urgent concerns into queued items is an operational observation rather than a finding, and it is not a criticism of the processes, which exist to prevent the loudest person from setting priority. The remedy of identifying the human owner is practitioner practice with no measurement behind it.",
  replication: "craft",
  idea: "Treat filing through the process as necessary and not sufficient, and find the person the process eventually routes to, because a queue absorbs urgency by design.",
  why: "Intake processes exist to stop priority being set by whoever shouts, and that is a good thing that you are about to work around, so it is worth being honest about what you are doing. The process is functioning correctly. It has taken your concern, assessed it against criteria it applies uniformly, and scheduled it. The problem is that its criteria do not know what you know, and there is no field on the form for the thing that makes yours different.\n\nSo the move is not to complain about the process, and it is not to file more tickets. It is to find the person who would eventually pick this up and give them the information the form could not carry, while leaving the ticket in place. Doing it in that order matters: you have followed the process, you are not asking anyone to skip it, and you are supplying context to the human it routes to. That framing is what stops this being queue-jumping, and it is also what makes it work, because the person you find has the standing to reprioritise and the form does not.",
  failureMode: "You wait for the process. Ninety days is longer than three weeks. The thing happens, the ticket is still open at a low priority, and the review notes that it had been raised through the proper channel, which is true and which made no difference to anything.",
  worked: "Acknowledge the process, do not attack it, and supply what the form could not:\n\n\"I have filed this properly, ticket 4471, and I am not asking you to jump the queue. I am contacting you because there is something the form could not capture.\n\nThe triage put this at ninety days, which is right for the category. What the category does not know is that the enterprise renewal for Henderson closes on the fourteenth, and this is the thing their security review flagged last year. If it is still open then, we do not get the renewal.\n\nThat is the whole message. If it still comes out as ninety days knowing that, I will leave it alone.\"\n\nIf it still comes out as ninety days knowing that, I will leave it alone is the sentence that makes this a supply of information rather than a demand, and it is one you have to mean.",
  experiment: "For anything you have filed that matters, find out who the queue routes to before you need to. Knowing the name in advance costs one question and it is the difference between escalating in an afternoon and escalating in a fortnight.",
  reflection: "What have you filed that is correctly queued and will arrive too late, and do you know who owns that queue?",
  recall: {
    q: "Why does a correctly functioning intake process still produce the wrong answer, and what is the right way around it?",
    a: "Because it applies uniform criteria that cannot know what makes your case different, and there is no field on the form for that information, so it is working properly and producing a wrong result. The right route is to leave the ticket in place, find the human the queue routes to, supply the missing context, and commit to accepting their answer if it does not change."
  },
  deepDive: "My concern is correctly queued and will be looked at too late. Help me write to the owner without it reading as queue-jumping."
},
{
  id: "escalation-false-alarm-budget",
  track: "escalation", level: "costly",
  title: "Price your own false alarms, because the people you escalate to are already doing it",
  source: "Alarm fatigue in clinical and industrial monitoring, where a high false-positive rate is documented to degrade response to true alarms",
  gateIntro: "You have escalated four things to the operations director this year. Three turned out to be nothing serious. The fourth is happening now and you believe it is real. You are drafting the message and you have noticed you are adding intensifiers, because you are aware of the three that were nothing.",
  gatePrompt: "Write it without intensifiers, and make the difference from the previous three explicit.",
  evidence: "Alarm desensitisation is extensively documented in clinical monitoring, where high false-positive rates are associated with delayed or absent response to genuine alarms, and it has been the subject of formal safety alerts by hospital accreditation bodies. The evidence is observational rather than experimental. Applying it to human escalation between colleagues is an analogy, and the analogy has an obvious limit: people, unlike monitors, can be told why this one is different.",
  replication: "craft",
  idea: "Recognise that your escalation history sets the prior on your next one, and correct for it with a discriminator rather than with intensity.",
  why: "The person receiving your fifth escalation has a base rate for you, whether or not they have thought about it, and yours is currently one in four. That prior is applied before your content. This is the same phenomenon as the monitor that alarms constantly and gets muted, and it operates without anybody deciding to discount you.\n\nThe instinct is to compensate with volume: intensifiers, urgency language, capital letters. That is the worst available response, because it is exactly what a false alarm looks like and it is indistinguishable from your previous three. The correcting move is a discriminator, which is a statement of what is different about this one that could be checked. My previous three were predictions and this is an observation is a discriminator. So is naming the specific evidence class: this one has customer-visible symptoms already occurring, the others were about what might happen. Unlike a monitor, the person you are escalating to can update on a discriminator, which is the one advantage human escalation has over an alarm system and the one most people never use.",
  failureMode: "You add the intensifiers. The message reads as the same register as the previous three, is weighted accordingly, and gets a response within the day rather than within the hour. The delay is the difference. You conclude you were not taken seriously and they conclude nothing at all, because nothing distinguished this message from the others.",
  worked: "Name the history, state the discriminator, and keep the register flat:\n\n\"I have escalated four things to you this year and three of them turned out to be nothing. I am aware of that and I would weight me accordingly.\n\nHere is what is different about this one. The other three were predictions about what might happen. This is already happening: forty two customers have had a wrong balance displayed since 09:20, and I have three screenshots. It is ongoing.\n\nI am not asking you to trust my judgement, I am telling you an observable thing, and the observation can be checked in about two minutes.\"\n\nOffering the two-minute check is what converts a credibility problem into a verification problem, and verification problems are the ones you can win.",
  experiment: "List every escalation you have made this year and mark which were real. If your rate is worse than one in two, work out what the false ones had in common, because that shared feature is your own filter miscalibrating and it is more fixable than the credibility.",
  reflection: "What is your escalation hit rate this year, and does the person receiving them know it better than you do?",
  recall: {
    q: "Why is adding intensity the wrong correction for a poor escalation history, and what should replace it?",
    a: "Because urgency language is exactly what a false alarm looks like and is indistinguishable from the previous ones, so it reinforces the prior rather than correcting it. Replace it with a discriminator: a checkable statement of what is different about this one, such as an observation already occurring rather than a prediction, since unlike a monitor a person can update on that."
  },
  deepDive: "I have a poor escalation hit rate and something real is happening. Help me write the discriminator that separates this from my previous false alarms."
},
{
  id: "escalation-when-you-were-wrong",
  track: "escalation", level: "costly",
  title: "Close out the escalations that turned out to be nothing, in public and quickly",
  source: "Practice in incident review and forecasting, where a resolved false positive is recorded rather than allowed to lapse",
  gateIntro: "Three weeks ago you escalated a concern about the queue depth to two levels above you. It was taken seriously, people spent time on it, and it turned out to be a monitoring artefact. Nothing bad was going to happen. Nobody has asked you about it since and the easiest available action is to say nothing and let it fade.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the closing message, sent to everyone who was on the original escalation.",
  evidence: "No trial. Recording the resolution of a false positive is convention in incident review and is central to any forecasting practice that scores itself, on the reasoning that a channel which only ever reports alarms and never reports their absence provides no information about its own reliability. Nothing measures the effect on how subsequent escalations are received.",
  replication: "craft",
  idea: "Announce the resolution when your escalation turns out to be nothing, promptly and to everyone who was involved, because it is the only way your escalations acquire a known reliability rather than an assumed one.",
  why: "An escalation channel that reports only alarms is uninformative about itself. If you raise things and never say what happened, the receiving end has no way to distinguish a person with a good hit rate from a person with a bad one, so they apply a generic discount to everybody. Closing out your own false positives is what supplies the missing half of the record, and it is the only thing that can make your escalations cost the recipient less over time.\n\nIt is also, uncomfortably, the thing that most people skip, and skipping it is legible. Letting a wrong escalation fade is a small act of reputation management that everyone involved can see: they spent time on your concern and heard nothing back. Doing it quickly and plainly has the opposite effect, and the size of that effect surprises people. Someone who escalates and then reports back when they were wrong is demonstrating that the escalation was about the risk rather than about being right, which is the precise thing the recipient cannot otherwise verify.",
  failureMode: "You let it fade. The two people who spent time on it remember an escalation that consumed a day and never resolved, and their prior on your next one moves in the wrong direction. You never find out this happened, because nobody mentions it, and the effect is applied silently to everything you raise afterwards.",
  worked: "Short, unhedged, no excuse-making, and with what you learned:\n\n\"Closing out the queue depth escalation from the third. It was a monitoring artefact, the queue was fine the whole time, and I was wrong.\n\nThanks for taking it seriously. The reason I got it wrong is that the dashboard aggregates across two clusters and I read it as one, which I did not know. I have added a note to the dashboard so the next person does not do the same thing.\n\nI would raise it again on the same evidence, and I would rather be wrong this way round.\"\n\nThe last line is not defensiveness. It states your policy explicitly, which is useful information for people deciding how to read your next escalation, and it is true.",
  experiment: "Go back through your escalations from the last year and find any that were never formally closed. Close them now, even the old ones. The lateness is slightly awkward and it is much better than the alternative, which is a set of open loops in other people's memories.",
  reflection: "Which of your escalations turned out to be nothing, and did you ever say so to the people who spent time on it?",
  recall: {
    q: "Why does failing to close out a false alarm damage you more than the false alarm itself, and what should the closing message contain?",
    a: "Because a channel that reports only alarms says nothing about its own reliability, so a generic discount is applied, and the people who spent time on it are left with an unresolved loop that moves their prior silently. The message should state plainly that you were wrong, explain the specific cause of the error, note any fix, and state that you would raise it again on the same evidence."
  },
  deepDive: "I escalated something that turned out to be nothing. Write the closing message, including what I got wrong and why."
},
{
  id: "escalation-problems-without-solutions",
  track: "escalation", level: "costly",
  title: "Bring your recommendation, and never withhold a problem because you have not got one",
  source: "The bring me solutions convention in management practice, and the countervailing argument in the organisational silence literature",
  gateIntro: "Your organisation has a strong norm, stated often by your director, that people should bring solutions rather than problems. You have found something wrong with the billing pipeline that you do not know how to fix and cannot even fully characterise. You have been sitting on it for four days while trying to construct a recommendation, and you are no closer.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the escalation you send today, with no solution in it.",
  evidence: "No trial supports the convention, which is management folklore repeated widely enough to function as policy. Against it, the organisational silence literature documents that a belief that speaking will change nothing, or that one must have a complete case, is among the most commonly cited reasons employees give for withholding concerns. That literature is interview and survey based rather than experimental, so the case here rests on weak evidence on both sides and on the asymmetry of the two errors.",
  replication: "contested",
  idea: "Attach your recommendation when you have one, because it is genuinely more useful, and report the problem anyway when you do not, because a norm that filters unsolved problems filters out exactly the ones nobody has thought about.",
  why: "The convention has a real point buried in it. An escalation that arrives with analysis and a proposal is more useful than one that arrives as an alarm, and the discipline of attempting a recommendation frequently resolves the issue before it needs escalating at all. So far so good, and that is why the norm is popular.\n\nThe failure is what it does when taken literally, which is that it applies a filter with exactly the wrong shape. Problems you can solve are, by construction, the ones already within somebody's competence and mental model. Problems you cannot solve are the novel ones, the cross-boundary ones, and the ones where nobody has yet worked out who owns it. A norm that admits the first category and blocks the second is filtering out the class that most needs organisational attention, and it does so invisibly, because the filtered problems produce no artifact. Your four days are that filter operating on you. The right position is to bring the recommendation when you have one and to name explicitly that you do not when you do not.",
  failureMode: "You keep working on a recommendation you cannot produce, because producing one is the condition for raising it. Nothing is reported. The problem is eventually discovered by its consequences, and the review finds that it had been known internally for some time, which is true and which the norm caused.",
  worked: "State the absence of a solution as a fact rather than an apology, and ask for the specific help you need:\n\n\"I am bringing you a problem without a solution, which I know is not how we usually do this, and I have spent four days trying to get past that and cannot.\n\nBilling reconciliation has a mismatch that I can reproduce and cannot explain. It is small, it is not obviously growing, and I do not know what causes it or whether it is customer-affecting.\n\nWhat I need is somebody who knows the settlement path, because I do not, and I do not know who that is. That is my actual ask: a name. I am not asking you to solve it.\"\n\nAsking for a name rather than for a fix is what makes this an efficient escalation rather than a handover, and it is frequently the only thing you actually needed.",
  experiment: "Ask your team directly whether anyone is currently sitting on something because they cannot construct a recommendation for it. Ask it as a neutral question rather than as a challenge to the norm. In most teams at least one person is, and their answer will tell you what your norm is costing.",
  reflection: "What are you currently holding because you cannot yet propose a fix, and how long has that been?",
  recall: {
    q: "What is right about the bring-solutions norm, and what is the specific shape of its failure?",
    a: "It is right that an escalation with analysis and a proposal is more useful, and that attempting a recommendation often resolves the issue first. It fails because it filters with the wrong shape: solvable problems are already within someone's competence, while unsolvable ones are the novel and cross-boundary ones that most need attention, and the filtered ones produce no artifact so the cost is invisible."
  },
  deepDive: "I have a problem I cannot solve or fully characterise. Help me write the escalation that asks for the right specific help."
},
{
  id: "escalation-retaliation-is-real",
  track: "escalation", level: "line",
  title: "Retaliation against people who report is common enough to plan for, and planning is not paranoia",
  source: "Repeated cross-sectional surveys of employee misconduct reporting, including the long-running US national business ethics surveys",
  gateIntro: "You are preparing to report something that will be unwelcome and that implicates someone senior. You have been told the organisation has a no-retaliation policy, and you believe the people telling you are sincere. You are trying to decide how much of your own preparation is prudence and how much is paranoia, and you have nobody to ask.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the sentence you use to tell one trusted person what you are about to do, before you do it.",
  evidence: "Large repeated surveys of employees in the United States have consistently found that a substantial minority of those who reported observed misconduct experienced some form of retaliation, with reported rates varying considerably by year and methodology and reaching well above one in five in some waves. These are self-report cross-sectional surveys with no verification of either the misconduct or the retaliation, and they say nothing about any particular organisation, including yours.",
  replication: "single",
  idea: "Prepare for the possibility of retaliation as a matter of routine rather than suspicion, because the base rate is high enough to warrant preparation and the preparation costs almost nothing if it turns out to be unnecessary.",
  why: "The survey evidence is imperfect and it is what exists. It is self-reported, it cannot verify either the original misconduct or the claimed retaliation, and it aggregates across organisations that differ enormously. What it does establish is that this is not rare, which is the only thing you need to know in order to justify preparation. You do not need to believe your organisation is bad. You need to notice that a substantial minority of reporters across many organisations experienced consequences, and that none of them expected to.\n\nThe preparation is unglamorous and cheap: tell one person outside your reporting chain before you report, so that somebody independent knows the sequence and the dates; keep your own dated record of what you reported and to whom; and know what the formal protections in your jurisdiction and organisation actually are rather than what you assume. None of that is hostile, none of it requires you to distrust anybody in particular, and all of it is worthless if you start it after something goes wrong. That asymmetry is the entire argument for doing it in advance.",
  failureMode: "You report without preparation because preparing felt like an accusation against colleagues you respect. Nothing overt happens, and over the following six months your scope narrows, a project moves, and a promotion cycle passes. None of it is attributable to anything, you have no dated record of the sequence, and you cannot tell whether you are seeing a pattern or constructing one.",
  worked: "Tell one person, factually, without asking them to do anything:\n\n\"I want to tell you something before I do it, and I am not asking you to get involved. On Thursday I am reporting the expenses issue to compliance. I have written down what I saw and the dates, and I have sent myself a copy.\n\nThe only thing I want from you is that somebody outside my reporting line knows I did this and when. If nothing happens, that costs you nothing. If something does, I would rather it was not just my word about the sequence.\"\n\nThe reason this works is that it asks for nothing except memory, which almost anybody will give, and which is the single most useful thing you can have.",
  experiment: "Before any report that implicates someone with power over your work, do the three things once: one person outside the chain, a dated record, and ten minutes finding out what the actual protections are. If it turns out to be unnecessary you have lost an hour.",
  reflection: "If you reported something serious tomorrow, who outside your reporting line would know you had done it?",
  recall: {
    q: "What does the survey evidence on retaliation establish and what does it not, and what does the preparation consist of?",
    a: "It establishes that retaliation against reporters is not rare across many organisations, from self-report cross-sectional data that cannot verify either the misconduct or the retaliation and says nothing about any specific organisation. Preparation is telling one person outside the reporting chain in advance, keeping a dated personal record, and knowing the actual formal protections, all of which are worthless if started afterwards."
  },
  deepDive: "I am about to report something that implicates a senior person. Walk me through the preparation I should do beforehand."
}
);
